<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Calculate SaaS Customer Lifetime Value</h2>
    <p>Customer Lifetime Value measures the overall gross revenue or net profit a single account generates before cancelling their subscription. SaaS executives use LTV alongside Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) to determine how much capital can be deployed into sales and marketing while maintaining profitable unit economics.</p>
    <p>Calculating your LTV requires three core metrics:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Average Revenue Per User (ARPU):</strong> The monthly recurring revenue earned per active customer account.</li>
        <li><strong>Monthly Churn Rate:</strong> The percentage of active subscribers who cancel or non-renew their subscription each month.</li>
        <li><strong>Gross Margin Percentage:</strong> The proportion of revenue remaining after subtracting direct operational costs such as server hosting, customer support, and payment gateways.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>The SaaS LTV Formulas</h2>
    <p>Gross Lifetime Value is determined by dividing ARPU by your monthly churn rate:</p>
    <p><code>Gross LTV = ARPU / Monthly Churn Rate</code></p>
    <p>For example, if your ARPU is $100 and your monthly churn rate is 2.5% (0.025), average customer lifespan is 40 months (1 / 0.025). Gross LTV equals $100 / 0.025 = <strong>$4,000.00</strong>.</p>
    
    <h3>Margin-Adjusted Net LTV Formula</h3>
    <p>To accurately measure gross profit contribution rather than top-line revenue, factor in your SaaS gross margin:</p>
    <p><code>Net LTV = (ARPU x Gross Margin %) / Monthly Churn Rate</code></p>
    <p>If your gross margin is 80%, Net LTV is $4,000 x 0.80 = <strong>$3,200.00</strong>. This figure represents the true margin cap for target CAC benchmarks.</p>
</section>

