<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>What is a healthy LTV to CAC ratio for SaaS?</h3>
    <p>A benchmark LTV to CAC ratio for growing software companies is 3:1 or higher. A ratio of 3:1 indicates that a customer generates three times more lifetime value than the cost required to acquire them, leaving sufficient margin for overhead, research, and expansion.</p>

    <h3>How does annual churn differ from monthly churn in LTV?</h3>
    <p>If you track annual churn instead of monthly churn, divide annual churn by 12 to approximate your monthly churn rate before running monthly LTV calculations. Alternatively, divide annual ARPU by annual churn to compute multi-year account value.</p>

    <h3>Why is gross margin critical when analyzing LTV?</h3>
    <p>Top-line revenue LTV ignores server hosting, third-party API dependencies, and customer success expenses. Margin-adjusted LTV ensures you do not overspend on customer acquisition relative to your true gross profit output.</p>
</section>

